00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.620
So starting with what--
what is Anthos Storage?

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.590
The key goal we're delivering
with Anthos Storage

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.620
is we're providing
enterprise-grade storage

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.970
management functions
for GKE workloads,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.820
whether those workloads
are running on-prem

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:45.990
or in the cloud.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.190
The goal is to be able to extend
the consistent experience,

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.860
the centralized control,
agility, and flexibility

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.300
that Anthos brings to the
table to the storage layer.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.420
And to do that, we're
working essentially--

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.710
our goal is to provide
customers with a consistent set

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.650
of control plane
interfaces where,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.620
independent of the vendor that
they're using for the data

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.580
plane, we want to provide a
set of APIs and interfaces

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.990
which are consistent.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.550
And to enable
that, we're working

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.083
with the community and our
partner community also,

00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:22.500
so a set of partners
we're working

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.020
can say how do we
engage and enroll them

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.320
to get that interface
adopted much more widely.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.840
So, it's a much
more broader effort

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.760
integrating with
community and the partners

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.300
and providing customers with a
seamless, consistent experience

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.750
for stateful
workload management.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.850
Now, getting into a
bit more specifics

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.920
about what are
the key use cases,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.010
the key functionality sets
we are planning to deliver,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.830
the first set of
functionality is

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.540
to enable stateful workloads.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.103
What we really look for is, if
I have to do storage management

00:02:55.103 --> 00:02:57.020
functions, especially
enterprise-grade storage

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.890
management functions
like snapshots,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.020
can I do it in
the same interface

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.945
that I am managing
my application?

00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:04.320
Or for those
functions, do I have

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.840
to go outside and enable those?

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.360
The goal for us is to
enable those functions

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.540
in the same interface,
the same place where

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.870
you are managing other
application-level functions.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.050
Other pieces we're
looking for here is--

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.050
well, if you're running
enterprise applications,

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.950
especially stateful
enterprise applications,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.690
how do you manage
disaster recovery

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:25.730
for those applications?

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:26.820
Can I take backups?

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.280
And what even backups
means in that world,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.880
is the code, data,
and all together,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.710
or it's just parts and
pieces at different rates.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:39.060
So one of the key goals is
to enable backups in GCS.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.060
And then you can rehydrate
them either on-prem

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.150
or in the cloud,
wherever you prefer

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.280
is the right place for the
workload in the application.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.050
Moving on, with any
stateful application,

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.340
one of the challenging
parts is upgrades.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.280
How do we enable
upgrades, and what happens

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.720
before you take an upgrade?

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.660
So being able to
enable local snapshots

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.060
of a stateful application--
that's another

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.510
set of functionality.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.830
And then, of course, security
and role-based access

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.710
is key to any
enterprise-grade application

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:14.560
or be ready for even enterprise
applications to come on board.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.890
Diving a bit more deeper,
so if you look at GCP,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.320
how we enable data
protection on GCP is you

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.740
can take a snapshot in
a multi-regional bucket

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.490
or a regional bucket in
GCS, which protects us

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:28.945
against either
regional failures,

00:04:28.945 --> 00:04:31.900
total failures across.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.500
Now, the goal is to work
with the partner community

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.680
so that the backups
can be stored in GCS.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.720
And depending on
the data locality

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.160
that the customer service
levels and the compliance pieces

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:48.370
allow, you will be able to
store those backups anywhere

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.220
in GCS, either a
regional a multi-regional

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.990
or a specific bucket bit on.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.900
So it's a close collaboration
with the partners

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.660
and the cloud to
enable the backup

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.180
and restore functionality.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.982
Now getting a bit
around why we started

00:05:05.982 --> 00:05:07.940
to solve this problem
and some some of the data

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.150
points we wanted to share as
we're talking to more customers

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:13.840
and partners.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.450
So, with the evolution
of Kubernetes,

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.730
we've gone from a
beginner-enthusiast phase

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.240
into Enterprise 1.0 and
moving towards a 2.0 adoption

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.790
where customers are
looking at Kubernetes

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.240
not just for stateless, but
also for stateful workload.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.980
And part of the
reason is they are

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.920
looking for, can I have
a consistent experience

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.800
and a standardized platform
for all my applications,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.350
versus some different
architectures

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.190
for different applications.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:41.610
And it's a move towards
that consistency

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.310
and being able to take the
advantage of Kubernetes

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.410
extend to stateful applications.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.380
But, again, as we get into it,
some of the basic functions

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.290
like key management,
app-consistent snapshots,

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.990
being able to take
cluster management-- those

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.690
are key areas that have to
be addressed consistently

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.890
across before we get to that.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.940
And as we move on, we're
looking to add functionality

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.320
around observability.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.470
If I have an SLO violation,
how soon can I address that?

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.260
Can I find all the moving
parts and pin them down

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.440
to say where is the choke
point, or how do I address that

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.240
within a reasonable time frame?

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.770
And last but not
least is getting

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.830
into how do we enable
management at scale

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:20.840
with a rich set of UIs.

00:06:24.365 --> 00:06:25.490
Couple of more data points.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:30.190
Now, there was a Google survey
done before GKE customers.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.740
Ended up a lot of
customers, around 39%,

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.610
are looking for a standard
lift-and-shift of applications.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.230
But if you look at
the numbers, there

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:42.250
is a larger set of
customers, close to 59%, that

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.400
are looking to modernize
the applications on-prem

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.920
and then move to the
cloud as and when

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.870
they become ready, versus
kind of just moving

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:55.340
that in the cloud and using
it as infrastructure pieces.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:58.600
So, our goal for
the Anthos Storage

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.940
is to address that
need where customers

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.920
are looking to
modernize applications

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:07.090
on-prem before they move to
the cloud and start scaling

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.000
or actually doing migrations,
whatever the use case may be.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.370
Now going deeper into what
are the specific challenges

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:18.890
that we see today with
stateful workloads.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:23.120
And one of the key challenges
we see is, for the most part,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.190
the storage that exists
today is designed

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.960
to be mapped to a host.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.650
And when you get into
Kubernetes world,

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.090
it's containers
and applications.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:35.400
So bridging that gap
has been a challenge.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.940
So that's where we're
working with the partners

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:40.100
to help enable bridge
that gap in a much

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.290
more standardized way.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.120
Next piece is, according
to a survey that

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:50.040
was done by Datadog last year,
the biggest difference is also

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.253
the average lifespan
of a container.

00:07:52.253 --> 00:07:53.670
An average lifespan
of a container

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.640
is two days, versus for
the VM, it's 23 days.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.620
Now, in that excel,
the data point

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.310
is interesting
with what it means.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:05.070
What it means is that, can I
move my data at the same pace

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.960
that my application is moving?

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.440
Can I match that pace?

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.710
And if I don't, what
risk does it create?

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:11.910
Do I have the backups?

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.030
Do I have the restore
points that if something

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.010
goes wrong during that mobility,
because it is so agile,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:23.740
can I restore my application
back in a consistent state?

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.620
So that's kind of
a key take away

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.410
from how we are
interpreting that statement.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.289
Now getting into basic
data lifecycle management,

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:34.260
can I create
app-consistent snapshots

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:35.970
when I'm running a DB inside?

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.429
What does it mean to take
an app-consistent snapshot?

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:40.943
Does it mean I take a
snapshot of the data?

00:08:40.943 --> 00:08:42.360
And if there is a
separate change,

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.420
do you take a snapshot
of code and config?

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.510
Or can I take a
consistent entity

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:51.760
with code, config, and
data, and make a snapshot

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.080
so when I take a snapshot
and I relocate it,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:57.090
I am assured that I can
rehydrate my application

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.230
and no components
are left behind?

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.770
So, these are some of
the issues we really

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.650
are seeing more and
more common as customers

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:09.870
are trying stateful applications
with Kubernetes GKE.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:12.270
Now getting a bit
deeper around what's

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:16.950
our approach to solve this
and how we're solving this.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.710
We understand storage
is very personal to what

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.100
workloads you're working on.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:24.300
This also depends on what
partners you choose to work on

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.050
given your workloads.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.260
So our intent is
to meet you where

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.000
you're comfortable
with the vendors

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.152
that you're comfortable
working with.

00:09:32.152 --> 00:09:36.050
With that spirit, we're working
with a large set of vendors.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.910
And listed here are some--

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.850
an initial set of storage
technology partners

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.540
that we already
started working with

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.530
and we're engaging to
solve those use cases.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.060
But this list will
grow over time.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:51.380
And our goal is to really
meet with the vendors--

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:54.290
with the partners that
you're comfortable with which

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:58.678
match the workload that
are deployed with the SLAs

00:09:58.678 --> 00:10:00.470
that you need for that
particular workload.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.280
So as you bring the
partners on board,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.870
really we also want to
create a level of comfort

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:14.750
by creating a
certification process.

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:18.200
And we're looking essentially
at four different types

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.300
of certifications
or validations.

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:22.100
One could be-- what
we're developing

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.230
is an open interface,
and partners

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:26.600
are welcome to write
to that interface.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.000
But once they write,
they can self qualify it,

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.480
and they can distribute
it to their channels.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.670
The next phase is that we, as
Google, test those drivers,

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.140
those softwares
in our lab to make

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.380
sure they work well
with Anthos environment

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.240
and things work seamlessly.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.160
And at that point, they
get a choice to say,

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:49.190
should we distribute
those through the vendor

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.470
infrastructure, or
should we distribute

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:53.990
those specifically like
software-defined vendors,

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:57.230
and also drivers for
appliance vendors?

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.660
Should we redistribute
them through Marketplace,

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.920
which gives customers a lot more
seamless, unified experience?

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.750
Eventually, as the
program matures,

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:07.760
we really want to
get to a point where

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.760
we are pre-packaging certain
sets of drivers in the bundle.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.100
So that gives one less click or
one less step for the customers

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.500
and the experience is
a lot more seamless.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.353
With this, I'll invite
Partha on the stage

00:11:23.353 --> 00:11:25.770
to talk more about some of the
customer challenges and use

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:26.750
cases.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:28.820
PARTHA SEETALA: Thank you, Manu.

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.553
So before I get started, let
me just see a show of hands.

00:11:31.553 --> 00:11:33.470
How many of you are
running stateful workloads

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.960
on containers today,
or Kubernetes today?

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.960
It's very impressive, actually.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.510
If you think about
it, just last year

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:44.930
I would say that very few
people [INAUDIBLE] their hands.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:46.950
And this is actually
an ongoing trend.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:48.890
Anyway, with that,
so who am I, and what

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.480
am I talking about today,
and what am I doing here?

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.890
So as a way of introduction,
my name is Partha Seetala.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:57.350
And I'm the Co-founder
and CTO of Robin.io,

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:02.090
and we are partners with
Google on Anthos platform.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.780
Now, a little bit
about the company.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.410
So the mission of
the company was

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:10.250
to enable our customers to
run mission-critical stateful

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.120
workloads on containers, and
by extension, Kubernetes.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.370
So when I say stateful
workloads, what does it mean?

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.540
I mean, it could be just
a small Jenkins job,

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:21.720
but persistent
volumes underneath it.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.750
But when we look at the market,
the real pain, if you will,

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:27.920
was around the more complex
platforms such as the big data

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.010
platforms that are Cloudera,
Elasticsearch, Splunk,

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.890
or of course, the NoSQL
platforms such as Mongo

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:38.090
and Cassandra and Couch,
and even traditional RDBMS

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.693
solutions like Postgres, Oracle
RAC, Con, and all that, right?

00:12:41.693 --> 00:12:42.860
And it's kind of surprising.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.530
If we look at it, nobody
really associates the word

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:46.910
"Oracle RAC" with Kubernetes.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.680
But we have production
deployments of that.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.320
So I think it's
safe to say that we

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.590
know a thing or two about how
you can run these applications

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:57.080
on Kubernetes and what
are the real challenges

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:02.090
that somebody is going to see
when you actually manage them.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:03.625
The one point I do
want to highlight

00:13:03.625 --> 00:13:05.750
is based on the experience
of the type of workloads

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:08.540
that we have been able
to onboard on Kubernetes.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.950
We have brought the expertise of
how you take all of this stuff

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:15.530
and then package it
into a set of APIs

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:20.840
that you could alert with
Google to give to the community.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:22.340
And our hope here
is that this would

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.220
become like what SQL
did to structured query,

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.390
this would do to data
management on Kubernetes

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:28.140
for stateful apps.

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.080
And that is the
thing that we want.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.000
And this API sets
to be something

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.760
that a lot of other
partners will also embrace.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.310
But what you're
going to see today

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:41.930
is the API specs and the
demo of how it looks like.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:44.720
But before we go there, what
is so different about running

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:45.530
stateful workloads?

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:48.130
So why do you even have to talk
about this as a main topic,

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:49.160
right?

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.570
So, a couple of things here.

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:53.540
So unlike a stateless workload,
which is very ephemeral

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:56.680
and the way you scale
it is very different

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:58.430
than, let's say, a
database where you just

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:00.560
don't add more parts
and take them away

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:03.440
because they are cost-associated
with data redistributions

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:04.073
and all that.

00:14:04.073 --> 00:14:05.490
So, that's on the
deployment side.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:08.360
But once deployed, unlike
a stateless application,

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:11.270
a stateful one has
a very long life.

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:14.660
And people are used to doing
data management on that.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.160
And when I say data
management, it's

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:17.950
usually around data
protection, whether it

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.757
is snapshots or backups or it
is around data collaborations

00:14:20.757 --> 00:14:23.090
along with their apps-- things
like clones and all that,

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:23.840
right?

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:25.640
But for the folks
in the room who

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.340
have come from the
storage background,

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.340
these are not new terms.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.330
I mean, everybody
understands these things.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:32.510
But the world is
very different today.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.220
In the previous world,
all these things

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:36.800
were usually done by the
storage administrator.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.630
In today's world, in
the Kubernetes world,

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.400
it's done by
developers and devops,

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.380
and they are not
necessarily storage experts.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.720
And it's very hard
for them to comprehend

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.850
what it means to
go snapshot a LUN

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:48.860
and what it means to
go and clone it, right?

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:51.890
So what you really need to do is
elevate this entire experience

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:56.410
into an application construct so
that developers can go and say,

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:59.000
I have a Mongo that I've spun
up either through an operator

00:14:59.000 --> 00:14:59.690
or through Helm.

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:00.710
And I want to do a snapshot it.

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:01.835
I don't want to back it up.

00:15:01.835 --> 00:15:05.190
I don't want to boot it from
one data center to the other.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.880
Now I'm going to go a
little deeper into what

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.100
it means to do those things.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.950
And so I'm going to use a
few of our real-life customer

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:15.450
experiences and pains
the customer the had

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:17.100
and how we had solved them.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.670
So the first thing is
our own data protection.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:23.600
So, you want to basically keep
a copy of your application

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:26.310
and its data in such a way
that if there is a corruption,

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:29.825
you can easily roll back to a
more cleaner, pristine state.

00:15:29.825 --> 00:15:31.200
Now, there are
two ways to do it.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.575
And it's really important to
kind of capture both of them

00:15:33.575 --> 00:15:34.880
and talk about the differences.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:37.430
There's this notion of
snapshots, which is usually

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.820
a vein which you can snapshot
your entire application

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:45.020
on the same disk from which
it is storing all the data.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:46.993
And then there is a
thing called backup,

00:15:46.993 --> 00:15:48.660
where you basically
take these snapshots

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.310
and then move it
up into the cloud.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:54.480
So both of them solve two
different needs, right?

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.900
However, if you look
at today's challenges,

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.150
the way you would do this--
let's say you're running--

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.792
I'll just take a very
simple example, Postgres.

00:16:01.792 --> 00:16:03.250
If you're doing
Postgres today, you

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:05.667
would have to go and tell your
storage administrator that,

00:16:05.667 --> 00:16:08.108
hey, listen, I want a
snapshot of my storage LUNs.

00:16:08.108 --> 00:16:09.150
They would snapshot that.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.160
But then what?

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.620
We just have a snapshotted LUN.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:15.680
What the developer
would want to do is they

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:18.740
would want to go and snapshot
not just the storage volumes,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:22.040
but all the material around
that storage volume, which

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.460
would be obviously the PVCs,
then things like ConfigMaps,

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:29.060
then StatefulSets, services,
secrets, all of that.

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.860
Because all of that essentially
forms your application.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.880
And once you can capture that
as an application snapshot,

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.210
then you can enable workflows
such as creating a quick clone

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:41.430
or migrating it to a different
data center and things

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:42.980
like that, right?

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:46.640
The next one is around clones,
so maybe a use case from one

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:47.920
of our Oracle RAC customers.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.060
So it's a large towels
bookings company.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:53.720
They run Oracle RAC
as a service on ROBIN.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:57.350
And one of the use case that
they have is their DBAs--

00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:59.832
at the end of every quarter,
they have to run reports.

00:16:59.832 --> 00:17:01.790
And they don't want to
go and run those reports

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:02.915
on the production database.

00:17:02.915 --> 00:17:03.717
So what do they do?

00:17:03.717 --> 00:17:05.300
They go to this
platform and they say,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.160
I want to create a
very quick clone.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.410
And they create a clone
that's not-- again, as I said,

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:10.819
it's not a storage clone.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:13.369
It is a fully
functional, running,

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.980
Oracle RAC instance
with the data

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:19.003
as of a previous
snapshot that was taken.

00:17:19.003 --> 00:17:20.420
Another use case
for a clone would

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:23.300
be to validate
patches and upgrades.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:25.790
Nobody wants to go and
run a patch or an upgrade

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:27.020
on the production database.

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:29.630
Either you like to create a
clone, run it there, validate.

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:31.920
If everything is fine, then you
would go and apply it, right?

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.080
So those are the
kind of use cases

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:34.850
that you can enable
and make it much more

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:36.860
easy to consume in this
world of Kubernetes

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:40.130
by elevating the entire
experience to applications.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:43.580
And the last
interesting use case

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.250
that I would like to
capture is app portability.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.300
And what I mean by
that is more and more

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:51.950
you'll see that developers want
to liberate their applications

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.920
and data from the underlying
infrastructure choices.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:57.750
You might start
today on-premises.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.000
Let's say all in AWS.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.378
But you would like to go migrate
tomorrow into Google Cloud

00:18:02.378 --> 00:18:04.920
because you have resources there
that you really want to use.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.020
Maybe you want to use
TPUs there, right?

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.140
So you want to have the
ability to go and migrate

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:11.720
your entire application, not
just the storage volumes.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:14.300
That's incredibly difficult
because each data center,

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:17.133
each cloud, has a different
way in which it does storage

00:18:17.133 --> 00:18:18.300
and networking and all that.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.050
So you could abstract
it out in such a way

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:23.340
that you can very
easily migrate things.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:26.842
Now, so, with this--

00:18:26.842 --> 00:18:28.550
I mean, these are the
kind of experiences

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:33.383
that we have been able to give
to our customers on Kubernetes.

00:18:33.383 --> 00:18:35.300
And this is the experience
that we have shared

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.270
and we code all of these APIs
with a very deep integration

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:40.705
with the Anthos Storage team.

00:18:40.705 --> 00:18:41.580
So what does it mean?

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.360
How would a partner's
life look like once we

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:46.610
have embraced these API sets?

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:48.950
So, I'm going to show you
what we have done at ROBIN.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:52.490
So let's look at this on
the left-hand side, before--

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.030
today, maybe, right?

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:56.540
Or you hear what is-- or what
I'm going to say on the right.

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.400
The way you deploy your
stateful workload is either you

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:02.870
would use the standard Kube
portal interface and use YAML,

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:04.760
or you will use a Helm
chart to deploy it,

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.100
or you might use an operator
if one exists, right?

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:08.350
So there is nothing different.

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:10.410
This is a very standard
way of doing things.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.140
But this is just provisioning.

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:17.320
What you need beyond this would
be something like this, right?

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.020
There you can go and
do an entire snapshot

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.760
of your Postgres, let's
say, Helm release, right,

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:25.867
or in running instance
of your operator.

00:19:25.867 --> 00:19:26.700
Or you can clone it.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:30.460
You can push it from
on-premises to GCS, right?

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:32.890
And then from GCS, you will
be able to drain it back

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:34.353
into a different region.

00:19:34.353 --> 00:19:35.770
So think of all
the power of this.

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:37.300
GCS is an object store.

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:38.817
Postgres sits on a block store.

00:19:38.817 --> 00:19:39.650
So what do you mean?

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:42.970
What we are doing here is from
one data center or one region,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.640
you're taking a snapshot.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.585
You're pushing that
snapshot from block.

00:19:46.585 --> 00:19:47.710
The snapshot sits on block.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:49.085
You push that
snapshot from block

00:19:49.085 --> 00:19:51.100
into GCS, which is object.

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:54.480
And you can go to a
completely different region.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:58.938
And using GCS's global
distribution of objects,

00:19:58.938 --> 00:20:00.730
you can go to a completely
different region

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:04.570
and rehydrate this data
from object into block

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:07.107
and offer it as a
running application.

00:20:07.107 --> 00:20:08.690
Now, imagine the
power of this, right?

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:11.260
So that is what this
API set is going to do.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:13.240
And we're hoping that
this does truly become

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.320
the SQL of data management.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.110
And we are very
honored to be a part

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:19.430
of this effort with Google.

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:22.120
With that, I'll hand it off to
Oleksiy for showing you a demo

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.260
and going into the
technical details.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.350
OLEKSIY STASHOK:
Thank you, Partha.

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:27.250
Hello.

00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:28.600
My name is Oleksiy Stashok.

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:33.060
I'm a Software Engineer
in Anthos Storage team.

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:37.980
And yeah, I'm
going to just stay.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:38.940
I think I have a mic.

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:40.440
PARTHA SEETALA: There you go.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:40.890
OLEKSIY STASHOK: OK.

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:42.557
So let's take a look
at the architecture

00:20:42.557 --> 00:20:44.560
and the high-level architecture.

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:48.780
So here, you can see
that we introduced

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:52.290
a couple of new components
to Kubernetes stage.

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:54.210
And every component
is represented

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:59.190
by this red block, which
is a component controller.

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:01.590
And the arrows pointing
towards the block

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:03.820
represent API for
the controller.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:07.950
The APIs are defined as
Kubernetes custom resource

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:10.110
definitions, CRDs.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:13.220
And you can notice that these
components are nicely layered

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:15.120
one on top of the other.

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:18.360
And so the top can
leverage functionality

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.500
of the component on the bottom.

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:23.130
So, let's take a look at
the individual components.

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.930
So, the volume group
controller and API

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:34.250
is responsible for taking
crash-consistent volume group

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:36.200
snapshots and backups.

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:41.420
It talks to CSI driver to run
some storage-level commands

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:44.780
like take a snapshot,
create a backup,

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:48.830
restore a volume from
a snapshot or backup.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:49.975
And it uses the GCS--

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:55.970
it uses GCS to
export the backups.

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:02.220
So, the next component is
application controller and API.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:03.660
And application
controller and API

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:06.180
are responsible for
taking application-level,

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:09.600
application-consistent
snapshots and backups, which

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:14.440
include both persistent data
and application metadata.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:20.610
And we use an application backup
management service to keep--

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.050
as a global register
for our backups.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:26.260
So the backups could be used
across different Kubernetes

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:26.760
clusters.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:33.830
And the third component is
application policy controller

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:37.670
and API, which is responsible
for scheduling and retention,

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:41.750
application-level snapshots and
backups, given some policies.

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:51.740
OK, at this point, I wanted
to just step back and explain

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:56.810
how we model our APIs,
what is the API structure,

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.920
and what's the responsibility
of each piece of the API.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:05.630
So we model our API the same
way as Kubernetes models

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:09.420
persistent volumes and
persistent volume snapshots,

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.860
which means we use a controller,
claim, and content triplets.

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:23.250
And their class provides
portability across clusters.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:26.950
And it refers to the specific
controller or handler

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:28.550
to handle claims.

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:32.120
And it also contains some
configuration for controllers--

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:34.720
say, some secret
keys or something

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:37.340
like opaque parameters
for controllers.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:42.890
And class usually is provided
by Kubernetes administrators.

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:45.590
The next one is a claim,
which is provided by a user.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:48.710
And actually, it's a request for
action, like take a snapshot,

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:50.740
create a backup.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:53.230
And the content is
the actual result

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.620
of the controller
processing the claim,

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:58.630
so, like, snapshot was taken.

00:23:58.630 --> 00:23:59.964
Here is a snapshot ID.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:04.970
OK, so in this
demo, we're mostly

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:07.140
talking about
application component.

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:08.780
And now we are
going to actually--

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:13.460
I'm going to just explain
the component's API and CRDs

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.630
we use in this component.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:17.840
So, first one is
stateful application,

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.400
which defines an
application which

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:21.410
has some persistent state.

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:24.890
You can use this CRD when
you want to just refer

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:26.630
to your existing application.

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:28.970
And later, you can refer
to the stateful application

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:31.430
when you take
snapshot or backup.

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:33.980
Or you can create
stateful application

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.220
and refer to existing snapshot
or backup in order to control--

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:39.380
to actually provision
you an application

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:40.760
based on the
snapshot and backup.

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:45.560
The next one's
application snapshot,

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:49.480
which represent the
application snapshot,

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:52.360
like state stored in
the local storage.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.090
And when we say "state," means
it's both persistent data

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:57.220
plus metadata.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:00.250
This here, it can be
used in two cases--

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:02.170
when you want to
actually snapshot

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:04.480
existing application
in your cluster,

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:08.750
or if you want to
import external backup

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:09.625
to the local storage.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:17.570
Application backup-- it
represents the application

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:21.680
state stored in external
storage, like in GCS,

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:22.665
for example.

00:25:22.665 --> 00:25:24.290
Again, here, we are
talking about state

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.890
which is represented by both
persistent data plus metadata.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:32.210
And application backups
are used either to back up

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.300
a running application
or in order

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:39.125
to export the local snapshot
to external storage.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:47.020
Application rollback defines a
user request to just rollback

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:49.120
existing application
to a previously taken

00:25:49.120 --> 00:25:50.065
snapshot or backup.

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.550
OK, here comes the demo.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.325
Go back to slides.

00:26:04.325 --> 00:26:05.271
Thank you.

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:12.400
So, I just wanted to touch
upon the use cases which

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:13.900
I'm going to cover in the demo.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:17.440
So, first use case is
rollback an application

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.950
after unsuccessful upgrade.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:22.470
And the second one,
we're going to migrate

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:26.700
the stateful workload from
on-premise Kubernetes cluster

00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:30.195
to the cloud Kubernetes cluster,
which is, in our case, GKE.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:34.100
The demo setup.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:37.060
So we actually have two
Kubernetes clusters.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:41.200
One is running on-prem
by GKE On-Prem.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:44.050
And we run it on VMware
and use the ROBIN

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:47.020
as the software-defined storage.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:50.920
In the cloud, we use GKE as a
provider of Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:55.180
And we also use ROBIN
software-defined storage

00:26:55.180 --> 00:26:57.790
for running on top
of persistent disks.

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:00.910
Our application consists
of two components,

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:03.640
is the UI, which
for simplicity we

00:27:03.640 --> 00:27:06.520
represent as just simple
SQLPad application, which

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:09.610
is just a web-based SQL tool.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:12.810
And persistent state is
stored in Postgres, which

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:15.450
we run in master-slave mode.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:18.380
And just to maybe add you some
color about the application,

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:22.000
so let's assume this is
a sales application we

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:24.217
run for our company's sales.

00:27:24.217 --> 00:27:25.800
But for the purpose
of the demo, we'll

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:29.310
only take a look at the part
of the application which

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:34.230
is responsible for
handling customers.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:38.303
OK, with that, we can
switch to the console.

00:27:38.303 --> 00:27:39.285
Thank you.

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:49.070
OK, so now, the
console where we--

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:54.850
the console on-prem, which you
can tell by this little blue

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:56.650
arrow at the bottom--

00:27:56.650 --> 00:28:00.360
I will switch from the
on-prem to the cloud

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:02.730
when we need it for
the second use case.

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:06.840
I prepared a set of
scripts, batch scripts.

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:08.580
Mostly, they are only like--

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:11.220
it's just they wrap a
cube couple commands.

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:13.020
So before running
every script, I'm

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.690
going to show its content
so we are on the same page

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:19.470
and explain what does
it do, what it does.

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:26.550
And yeah, so let's take a look
at the application which is

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:28.020
running in the on-prem cluster.

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.460
So, we see, as I mentioned,
it's a sales application

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:35.090
which consists of two parts.

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:38.290
The first one is the
component of Postgres

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:44.390
running in master-slave mode,
and the actual UI we have here.

00:28:44.390 --> 00:28:49.170
So let's take a look at
the UI and the content

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:51.088
and the data we have
in the database.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:10.250
So, we have a customers
table, which has--

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:13.740
for every customer, we have
first name, last name, email,

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:14.240
and company.

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:22.750
So, let's run some
prerequisites.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:25.225
First, prerequisite script.

00:29:25.225 --> 00:29:29.390
But let me show you what it has.

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:33.290
In this script, we just deploy
ApplicationSnapshotClass

00:29:33.290 --> 00:29:35.930
and ApplicationBackupClass.

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:40.660
In the snapshot class,
we define that ROBIN is

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:43.290
going to handle the snapshots.

00:29:43.290 --> 00:29:45.290
And we say that--

00:29:45.290 --> 00:29:50.180
and here, we defined also
Postgres quiesce and unquiesce

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:50.720
hooks.

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.595
So we are able to take
application-consistent

00:29:52.595 --> 00:29:53.910
snapshots.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:57.433
In the backup class, we defined
just the backup handler who's

00:29:57.433 --> 00:29:58.600
going to handle our backups.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:02.640
OK, let's run the script.

00:30:09.882 --> 00:30:12.340
So, the next step would be--
we have a running application.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:16.220
Now let's create a stateful
application on top of it.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:21.490
So we are going to run
the second script where we

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:23.620
defined a stateful application.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.660
And we refer to the
sales application

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:27.286
which we are currently running.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:36.930
So now we're going
to wait a little bit

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.450
until controller sees that
request and fulfills it.

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:45.010
OK, so we registered a stateful
application of the controller.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:50.310
OK, so now it's time
for the first use case.

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:56.610
It's a rollback after
unsuccessful upgrade.

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:58.370
Yeah, so, here is a story.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:03.710
Assume you have PM come
in, approaching you.

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:06.620
Like, PM-- let's just say, like,
Manu asked me to specifically

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:08.300
say it's not him.

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:09.730
So it's not Manu.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:13.100
Yeah, so, we are
on the same page.

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:14.970
Yeah, so, where was I?

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:18.030
Yeah, so, Manu approaches
me and says, OK,

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:22.280
so sales team has
new requirements.

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:28.310
And they want actually
to extend the way

00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:30.200
we maintain the company's data.

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:33.870
So now we have company name
only as part of the customer,

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:36.260
but they want to actually
extend that functionality

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:39.410
and maintain more information
about the company.

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:41.525
And I said, yeah, that's cool.

00:31:41.525 --> 00:31:42.650
I need three days for that.

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:44.490
He says, nice.

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:45.600
You have one day.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:46.190
I got to go.

00:31:46.190 --> 00:31:47.540
Bye.

00:31:47.540 --> 00:31:49.100
OK.

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:53.000
Now we have one day to
actually do two changes, right?

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:55.010
We need to change
the UI and we need

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:58.560
to actually provide the
change to the database schema.

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.410
So we can extract the info
from the customers table--

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.780
we need to extract the
information of company

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:08.690
and put that into
a separate table.

00:32:08.690 --> 00:32:11.720
OK, so I prepared an
upgrade script for that.

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:14.720
But considering it
didn't have much time

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:18.650
for testing and it's usually--

00:32:18.650 --> 00:32:21.320
it's, like, database
schema change is usually

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:22.050
pretty dangerous.

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:24.260
So we are going to
take a snapshot first

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:26.226
of the existing application.

00:32:26.226 --> 00:32:29.760
Now let me show you what we do.

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:33.530
So, we are creating an
application snapshot

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:35.652
and refer to the application--

00:32:35.652 --> 00:32:37.610
the stateful application
which we are currently

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:39.890
running, which we registered
in the previous step.

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:57.180
OK, so, controller
started working on it.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:02.040
So just to stress it,
so when we say, like,

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:06.390
take an application snapshot
and take an application backup,

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:09.870
we are talking about both
persistent data and metadata.

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:12.152
OK, snapshot is ready.

00:33:12.152 --> 00:33:16.360
Everything is ready
for the upgrade.

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:17.370
So let's do the upgrade.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:24.760
OK, this is our current
state of the application.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:27.100
As you see, we use
UI version 1.0.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:30.120
And we're going to
upgrade it to version 2.0.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:33.440
And here is the rest
of the application.

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:35.070
So we are ready to continue.

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:40.180
Here is the script, which will
perform the actual database

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:40.960
upgrade--

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:42.370
schema upgrade.

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:43.330
So, are you sure?

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:44.307
Yes, I am.

00:33:50.768 --> 00:33:53.280
Got to just wait
for the upgrade.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:54.685
It's going to be really soon.

00:33:58.840 --> 00:33:59.340
Done.

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:00.040
OK.

00:34:00.040 --> 00:34:06.140
So now, please notice that we
have UI version 2.0 running.

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:10.940
And let's give some
time for the application

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:14.762
to settle down so it's still,
like, UI's terminating.

00:34:17.996 --> 00:34:19.350
Come on, come on, come on.

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:19.850
Yeah.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:20.489
OK.

00:34:20.489 --> 00:34:26.230
So upgrade-- I would say
it was successful, right?

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:26.989
No error.

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:28.239
Let's take a look at the data.

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:44.330
Right, you can see that now
we have three tables, not only

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:44.830
one.

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:46.663
So let's take a look
at the company's table.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:54.330
Right, so company
names were successfully

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:57.767
transferred from the customer's
table to the new table.

00:34:57.767 --> 00:34:59.100
Let's take a look the customers.

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:07.387
OK, yeah, please
notice what happened.

00:35:07.387 --> 00:35:08.970
Instead of-- like,
during the upgrade,

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:11.160
instead of extracting
the company

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:14.460
and then deleting this
column, I actually

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:17.730
deleted the email
column from the table.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:20.550
But fortunately, we have
our snapshot, right?

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.990
So now we are able to
rollback our application

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:26.850
to the previous state,
fix the upgrade script,

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:27.570
and run it again.

00:35:30.220 --> 00:35:35.270
So now let's rollback
the application.

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:41.820
So I'm going to run this script,
which defines the rollback--

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:44.940
points to the sales
stateful application,

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:46.950
and the source says
use a snapshot which

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:48.240
we took on the previous step.

00:35:51.127 --> 00:35:52.210
So let's do that rollback.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:58.360
So the rollback will
take a little bit

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:01.740
longer because as a
rollback, as I understand it,

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:05.140
it's a complete, full
application rollback, including

00:36:05.140 --> 00:36:09.250
its pods and everything because
metadata was also stored.

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:11.170
So as part of this
upgrade, we will again

00:36:11.170 --> 00:36:15.610
switch back from UI version
2.0 to UI version 1.0.

00:36:15.610 --> 00:36:19.370
So now you see that all
the pods are terminating.

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:21.370
So we need to wait until
they are all terminated

00:36:21.370 --> 00:36:24.910
and they will be replaced
with the snapshot

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:30.820
version of these application.

00:36:30.820 --> 00:36:32.470
So, UI is always behind.

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:35.100
So I need to do
something about that.

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:42.480
OK, OK, OK.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:42.980
Done.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:47.870
So, the new version
is terminated.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:49.760
And now you'll
see that the older

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:51.860
one, the snapshotted
version, will actually

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:53.550
start spinning up.

00:37:04.935 --> 00:37:11.370
And here.

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:16.170
OK, so please know that
now UI is version 1.0.

00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:19.860
So it's a previous
version of our UI.

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:24.310
And soon, we're going to have
all the pods running and ready.

00:37:24.310 --> 00:37:26.430
And so we can go
back to the database

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:28.250
and see what's in there.

00:37:36.478 --> 00:37:39.156
OK, let's take a
look at the database.

00:37:53.692 --> 00:37:55.150
You see now we have
only one table.

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:04.440
And all our information is
back, so including emails.

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:09.700
So now we can fix the
upgrade and run it again.

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:13.710
So this concludes
our first use case.

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:17.460
And the second use case
is going to be migrating

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:20.580
our stateful workflow from
on-premise Kubernetes cluster

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:21.090
to GKE.

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:27.000
So, as a first step,
we need to actually--

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:30.090
now we have this local snapshot
we took before the upgrade.

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:32.880
Now we need to actually
make this snapshot

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:35.340
available for the
external cluster.

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:37.940
To do that, we actually
export this snapshot

00:38:37.940 --> 00:38:43.680
to include in data and metadata
to GCS using this script.

00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:52.470
So we are creating
application backup

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:54.470
as a source to provide a
snapshot, which we took

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:56.100
in one of the previous steps.

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:59.880
And the target location
we say is the GCS bucket.

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:03.390
And the secret we provide
to give controller access

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:04.690
to the bucket.

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:05.690
So let's run the script.

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:16.150
Usually, taking backups may
take time because it's like--

00:39:16.150 --> 00:39:19.690
we transfer data from local
storage to the remote one.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:21.950
But, in our case, we
don't have a lot of data.

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:26.320
And usually our
storage providers--

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:30.640
they support incremental
backups, which means,

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:36.635
once you upload it-- like,
full backup, the next updates

00:39:36.635 --> 00:39:38.260
to the backup will
be just incremental,

00:39:38.260 --> 00:39:41.540
so only the deltas will be sent.

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:42.880
So, done.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:43.990
So, OK.

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:47.210
The backup was exported.

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:50.390
And we have this backup
ID which we'll need later

00:39:50.390 --> 00:39:54.020
to actually import information
about the backup on the GKE

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:55.420
cluster.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:56.600
OK, let's switch to GKE.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:05.605
I'm going to switch the
kubectl context to GKE.

00:40:12.722 --> 00:40:13.532
Done.

00:40:13.532 --> 00:40:15.615
Let's take a look what we
have in the GKE cluster.

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:18.940
Nothing.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:19.800
That's good.

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:22.780
Exactly what we need.

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:23.950
And, yeah.

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:26.690
So the first step, now
when we are in GKE cluster,

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:30.550
we want to import knowledge
about the external backup

00:40:30.550 --> 00:40:34.580
to internal Kubernetes cluster.

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:38.880
So for that, we'll
need the ID I was--

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:42.217
actually, let's take a look
first at the import script.

00:40:45.508 --> 00:40:47.050
So as you see, we
are going to create

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:50.560
the application backup, which
refers to the external handle.

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:53.210
It's the ID we obtained
in the previous step

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:56.410
when we exported the backup.

00:40:56.410 --> 00:40:59.170
And as a location for
where we take the data,

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:04.270
it's GCS bucket, which is the
